   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "A particle moves along the polar curve \\( r = 2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\) so that its angle at time t is \\( \\theta(t) = \\frac{t}{2} \\). Using a calculator, find the rate at which the particle's distance from the origin is changing with respect to time at the instant when \\( \\theta = 0.4 \\). Show the setup for the calculation, and give the value correct to three decimal places.",
      "setup_required": true,
      "command_verb": "find"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "numeric",
      "mathjson": -1.4347121817990456,
      "decimals": 3
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "The distance from the origin is r, and by the chain rule \\( \\frac{dr}{dt} = \\frac{dr}{d\\theta}\\cdot\\frac{d\\theta}{dt} \\), with \\( \\frac{dr}{d\\theta} = - 4 \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} \\).",
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99049",
         "rule_named": "chain rule"
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At \\( \\theta = 0.4 \\), a calculator in radian mode gives \\( \\frac{dr}{d\\theta} \\approx -2.869 \\).",
         "mathjson": -2.869424363598091,
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99005",
         "rule_named": "evaluation at the stated angle"
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Since \\( \\theta(t) = \\frac{t}{2} \\), \\( \\frac{d\\theta}{dt} = \\frac{1}{2} \\). So \\( \\frac{dr}{dt} \\approx -2.869 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\), which is about -1.435.",
         "mathjson": -1.4347121817990456,
         "point_type_id": "BC-PT-99004",
         "rule_named": "multiply by the rate of the angle"
      }
   ],
